 .
 v2.6.0 fixes the blank attack map and charts after login: the login gate
 hides the whole layout, so the map and every canvas were measured at 0x0
 and never re-measured — Leaflet cached the zero size and each chart's
 small-size guard dropped its frame with no retry, leaving a manual page
 refresh as the only cure. Login now re-measures on the next frame, and a
 ResizeObserver re-draws anything returning from zero size, covering view
 switches too. Also: accessibility and theming pass — prefers-reduced-motion
 support that stops decorative animation while keeping progress spinners
 alive (and without hiding attack-map markers, whose keyframes end at
 opacity 0), light-theme contrast fixes bringing all 8 stat colours and all
 15 nav colours to WCAG AA 4.5:1 (several were as low as 1.9:1), an
 undefined --fg variable that silently broke three rules, a duplicate
 .icon-btn block, and a redrawn Ubuntu mark that no longer vanishes on
 light themes.
 .
 v2.6.1 smooths the CPU, memory and bandwidth graphs. The Catmull-Rom
 control-point coefficient was 0.4 where the maths calls for 1/6, so the
 curve overshot every sample and invented up to ~24px of vertical wobble
 that was never in the data; control points are now also clamped to each
 segment's y-range, which makes overshoot impossible rather than merely
 small. Dense series (300 bandwidth samples across a ~790px card, under
 3px apart) additionally get a centred 3-tap pass, symmetric so peaks keep
 their position in time. Together these cut line travel by ~63% and
 zigzag reversals by ~67%; single-sample peaks are damped by about 5%
 in the drawing only — numeric readouts still show raw samples.
 .
 v2.7.0 is a security release fixing broken access control. The read-only
 "viewer" role was enforced only by hiding controls in the browser: of 101
 POST endpoints just 9 could check role at all, so any signed-in viewer
 could reboot the router, rewrite firewall rules, read WireGuard peer
 configs, restore backups, or drive the AI agent — which runs shell
 commands and holds sudo — while also being able to approve the agent's
 own permission prompts. Role gating is now default-deny: admin is
 required unless an endpoint is explicitly listed, viewers keep only
 self-service password and TOTP actions, and /api/backups/** and
 /api/agent/** are admin-only on GET too (raw backups contain auth.json
 and WireGuard private keys). Also fixes /api/system/self-restart, whose
 loopback check read client_address — always 127.0.0.1 behind nginx
 proxy_pass, leaving it reachable unauthenticated from the LAN or VPN;
 it now resolves the real peer via nginx's X-Real-IP. The AI Assistant
 is hidden from non-admins in the UI to match the server-side rule.
 .
 v2.7.1 fixes the dashboard returning a blank page over IPv6. The nginx
 site listens on [fd00:1::1]:80 but its access lists allowed only
 127.0.0.1, ::1 and the RFC1918 IPv4 ranges, so any client arriving over
 IPv6 hit "deny all" and, via error_page 403 =200, received an empty
 200 rather than an error. It stayed hidden because bare-IP access is
 IPv4-only; it appears as soon as the dashboard is reached by a hostname
 carrying an AAAA record, when browsers prefer IPv6. All four access
 lists now also allow fd00::/8, covering the fd00:1::/64 LAN and the
 fd00:2::/64 WireGuard range. fd00::/8 is used deliberately rather than
 a delegated global prefix, which changes whenever the ISP rotates it.
 Served by nginx from /var/www/html, backed by a Python API server running
 as the systemd unit tuxwall.service on port 8008.
 .
 v2.7.2 adds an Avahi mDNS reflector for Music Assistant / AirPlay /
 Chromecast discovery across segments. mDNS is link-local, so without a
 reflector Home Assistant on one segment cannot discover players on another
 (LAN <-> WireGuard VPN). setup.sh, the first-boot wizard and postinst now
 install avahi-daemon, set enable-reflector=yes under [reflector], deny the
 WAN interface so mDNS never leaks to the internet, and allow UDP 5353
  inbound on LAN/VPN interfaces only. Also: opencode agent gains bounded
  service-control sudo rights (named restarts/reloads, tc, speedtest and
  tuxwall's own scripts — no blanket admin) with matching permission prompts.
  .
  v2.7.3 fixes the missing Ban/Unban buttons in the Security page's Top
  Attackers table: the CrowdSec availability and blocklist checks ran at
  page load while the login gate was still up, so both API calls failed
  unauthenticated and left the dashboard with CrowdSec marked unavailable —
  every later re-render then drew an empty Actions cell, making a manual
  page refresh (with a valid session cookie) the only way to get the
 buttons back. The ban state is now re-fetched after login and every time
 the Security view is shown, so the buttons appear on their own.
 .
 v2.7.4 rebrands the dashboard with the new wide TuxWall banner logo on
 the login page and in the sidebar (redundant text headings hidden since
 the banner carries the wordmark; status on the left, version on the
 right underneath). Also: guided SQM speedtest no longer needs the snap
 or Ookla's apt repo (whose packagecloud builds end at jammy and 404 on
 noble+) — setup.sh and the .deb postinst now auto-install the official
 Ookla static tarball via scripts/install-speedtest.sh (best-effort; the
 dashboard keeps working with manual caps and an install hint when the
 binary is absent).
 .
 v2.7.5 adds community ban reporting to tuxwall.org. The dashboard now
 auto-reports every real ban (CrowdSec captures and manual blocklist
 entries, each tagged with a category) to the public community ban
 database at tuxwall.org, and the Security page's Top Attackers table
 replaces its AbuseIPDB lookup with a green "community hits" badge — the
 aggregate number of events the community has reported for that address,
 linking to a per-IP report card (geo, ASN, category breakdown and event
 timeline) on tuxwall.org. Community lookups run once per address with a
 five-minute cache that survives table redraws, and the collector ingest
 is delta-based so re-reporting the same IP never double-counts its hits.
 Bundled with the collector deployment tooling (deploy-collector.sh,
 tuxwall-collector systemd unit and nginx vhost). Also: the dashboard
 nginx site no longer heuristic-caches index.html, so asset-version
 bumps take effect on the first reload instead of relying on a browser
 hard-refresh, and the deploy script now syncs the stylesheet into the
 webroot (a previous release updated the repo CSS but never shipped it
 to /var/www/html).
 .
 v2.7.6 reworks the Top Attackers community button on the Security page.
 It was a bespoke green pill showing a shield glyph and the community hit
 count, matching nothing else in the UI and spending most of its life on a
 placeholder while the lookup was in flight. It now uses the standard
 button chrome, shows an info glyph instead of a number, and takes its
 accent colour from that IP's community severity — low green, medium
 amber, high red, critical purple — with unreported, loading and
 API-unreachable states rendering neutral grey so "no data" never reads
 as "safe". The tuxwall.org lookup is retained (same five-minute cache and
 in-flight dedupe) but drives colour only, and the severity string is
 whitelist-checked before it reaches the DOM; tiers come from the
 collector's own severity_for(), so the dashboard and tuxwall.org cannot
 disagree. Also bumps the index.html asset-version stamps, which the .deb
 had never done: postinst ships index.html verbatim, so package upgrades
 carrying dashboard JS or CSS changes left browsers running the cached
 previous assets until a manual hard-refresh (setup.sh already stamped
 them, the package path did not).
 .
 v2.7.7 enriches community ban reports with Suricata evidence. Reports to
 tuxwall.org previously carried only how an IP was banned locally (manual
 crowdsec ban -> "other", blocklist add -> "blocklist"), so the community
 report cards' category breakdown never reflected the observed abuse type.
 Bans reported now consult the dashboard's Suricata alert tail: the most-
 alerted abuse category (bruteforce, scan, ssh, web, rdp, dos, malware,
 spam) replaces the generic one, the top matching signature is appended to
 the report reason inside the collector's 96-char cap, and the top five
 signatures with alert counts are attached to the report payload.
 Informational signatures (ET INFO, ET POLICY, ethertype and protocol-
 decode noise) are excluded so benign traffic is never mislabelled.
 The tuxwall.org report cards render a "Suricata signatures observed"
 card from this evidence, and the per-IP JSON API exposes it; the
 collector keeps per-signature counts as a max across re-reports so they
 can never be inflated. Also bundles tuxwall.org collector deployment
 tooling (not shipped in this package) for loopback-only, key-gated admin
 endpoints to list and purge community bans — the removal path for
 mistakenly reported IPs.
 .
 v2.7.8 adds UFW drop evidence to ban reports when Suricata has no
 signature. Suricata only alerts on what a signature describes — a
 well-formed SYN to a closed port matches nothing, and that is most of
 what a home firewall actually receives, so the v2.7.7 signature
 enrichment stayed silent for nearly every real attacker (observed
 here: zero alerts for all nine community-banned IPs while the eve log
 held 229k LAN-noise alerts). When no signature evidence exists, the
 report now falls back to the UFW drop record — the log that provably
 sees these attacks: the drop pattern is summarised into the report
 reason ("port scan: 29 ports probed (top: 5800, 5900, ...)" or
 "telnet probe: 150 packets to port 23") and the category maps off that
 text, so the tuxwall.org report card shows the observed abuse type for
 plain scans and probes too. Suricata keeps priority when it does have
 evidence; the firewall packet count also fills in for hits when the
 security monitor's window has no entry for the IP.
